AB  -  Objective: To observe the clinical effects of transcutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation (TEAS) with different acupoint combinations on analgesia, sedation and prevention of induced abortion syndrome in induced abortion, and to explore the best acupoints for transcutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation in induced abortion Compatibility scheme. Methods: Select 200 patients who voluntarily requested artificial abortion, and randomly divided them into group I (Sanyinjiao + Zusanli), group II (Sanyinjiao + Diji), group III (Sanyinjiao + Taichong), group IV (lidocaine Cervical block anesthesia), group V (blank control, without any analgesic measures), 40 cases in each group. Group Ⅰ, Ⅱ, and Ⅲ selected Sanyinjiao as the main acupoint, respectively compatible Zusanli, Diji, and Taichong points, and performed percutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation 30 minutes before the operation until the end of the operation. Observe the hemodynamic parameters such as mean arterial pressure, heart rate, blood oxygen saturation and intraoperative blood loss in the 5 groups of patients during the operation; evaluate the visual analogue pain scale (VAS) during operation and 30 minutes after operation and the Ramsay score during operation. Sedation score; record the cervical laxity and the occurrence of induced abortion syndrome during the operation. Results: The VAS score and Ramsay score during operation and 30 minutes after operation: there were statistically significant differences between group Ⅰ, group Ⅱ, group Ⅲ, group Ⅳ and group Ⅴ (P<0.05). Conclusion: Transcutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation can significantly reduce pain scores during and after 30 minutes of induced abortion, and has a good sedative effect; the combination of Sanyinjiao and Diji acupoints can relieve pain, sedation, dilate the cervix and prevent Abortion syndrome works best.
