AB  -  Objective: To evaluate the effect of transcutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation (TEAS) on the serum level of S100‐â as well as NSE for patients undergoing radical thoracoscopic lung cancer operation and their postoperative cognitive dysfunction (POCD). Method: Sixty patients scheduled for elective thoracoscopic lung cancer operation were divided into two groups by randomized digital table, while patients in group N were treated without TEAS and patients in group E with TEAS on Neiguan (PC6) and Zusanli (ST36) 30 min before anesthesia induction until the end of the operation and we observed the different levels of serological indicator of NSE as well as S100‐â after operation and POCD between the patients of the two groups. Results: Compared with group N, the serum level of NSE as well as S100‐â and the incidence rate of POCD in group E was significantly lower (P<0.05). Conclusion: Transcutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation on Neiguan (PC6) and Zusanli (ST36) can reduce the incidence rate of POCD in patients undergoing radical thoracoscopic lung cancer operation.
