AB  -  Background: Infertile women undergoing in vitro fertilization (IVF) are usually stressful, but most of them are undiagnosed and untreated. There is negative correlation between the anxiety levels and the IVF outcomes. We aim to explore whether transcutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation (TEAS) can relieve anxiety levels of infertile women and improve IVF outcomes. Case summary: A total of 360 infertile women with tubal blockage were randomized into four groups with a proportion of 1:1:1:1: control group, TEAS‐2 Hz group, TEAS‐100 Hz group and TEAS‐2/100 Hz group. After patients who did not complete embryo transfer were excluded, 84, 84, 80 and 86 cases were in the above groups respectively. The anxiety levels were rated with Spielberger's State Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI) and the Amsterdam Preoperative Anxiety and Information Scale (APAIS). Data of clinical pregnancy rate (CPR), implantation rate (IR) and live birth rate (LBR) were obtained for the patients. Outcomes: We found 2/100 Hz TEAS treatment significantly decreased the levels of state anxiety and preoperative anxiety of the patients (P < 0.05). The CPR, IR and LBR of the TEAS‐2/100 Hz group were significantly higher than those of the other three groups (P < 0.05). Implications: TEAS using a frequency of 2/100 Hz could help to alleviate anxiety levels and improve IVF outcomes.
