AB  -  INTERVENTION: Trial group:After admission, patients received routine clinical nursing care, including ice application, elevation and immobilization of the affected limb, maintenance of cast or brace fixation, and close monitoring of distal sensation and circulation of the injured limb. Starting from the first day of hospitalization, transcutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation was performed, targeting the acupoints Yanglingquan (GB34), Qiuxu (GB40), Yinlingquan (SP9), and Shangqiu (SP5). The electrical stimulation frequency was set at 2/100 Hz sparse‐dense wave, with an intensity of 5–10 mA, adjusted to a level where patients felt a sensation of soreness, numbness, and distension at the acupoints. Each session lasted 30 minutes, administered twice daily (in the morning and afternoon at fixed times), and continued for 3 consecutive days.;Control group:After admission, patients received routine clinical nursing care, including ice application, elevation and immobilization of the affected limb, maintenance of cast or brace fixation, and close monitoring of distal sensation and circulation of the injured limb. Starting from the first day of hospitalization, placebo‐controlled intervention was performed at fixed times in the morning and afternoon, with the same stimulation sites, pattern, and duration as the experimental group. The stimulation intensity was set at 0.5 mA, a weak current that produced only a mild skin sensation.; CONDITION: Ankle fracture PRIMARY OUTCOME: Pain score;Degree of swelling;The swelling of the foot; SECONDARY OUTCOME: Postoperative Complications;Length of stay; INCLUSION CRITERIA: 1. Unilateral ankle fracture was diagnosed by X‐ray, MRI, CT or other imaging examinations. 2. Age >=18 years old. 3. Fresh fracture, fracture to admission time <=24 hours. 4. Open reduction and internal fixation were planned. 5. Signed informed consent.
