AB  -  Objective: The effect of percutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation on the prevention and treatment of hyperalgesia after remifentanil anesthesia Methods: A total of 60 patients who received radical mastectomy under general anesthesia in our breast surgery department in August 2012 ‐2013 February were randomly allocated to the treatment group (group A) and the control group (group B). The patients in the treatment group 30 min before induction of anesthesia, Hegu, Neiguan Liangqiu accepted transcutaneous electrical stimulation continued till the end of the operation. The frequency of electric stimulation was 2 Hz and continuous wave, and the current intensity was adjusted properly with the patient's tolerance, and it was continuously stimulated to the end of the operation. The control group placed electrodes at the corresponding acupoints but did not connect the power supply. Two groups of patients were treated with intravenous inhalation combined anesthesia, continuous infusion of remifentanil 0.2 mu g/ (kg. Min), and adjusting the inhalation of seven halothane to adjust the depth of anesthesia. Postoperative recovery time, mean arterial pressure, heart rate, pulse oxygen saturation and Visual Analogue Scale at 1 h after resuscitation were recorded and compared. The incidence and severity of postoperative hyperalgesia in two groups were evaluated. Results: There was no significant difference in recovery time between the two groups (P > 0.05). Compared with the control group, the heart rate and mean arterial pressure of 1 h after treatment in the treatment group were significantly lower than those in the control group. The difference was statistically significant (P < 0.05). There was no significant difference in the difference of pulse oxygen saturation between the two groups (P > 0.05). The scores of 1 h and 5 h VAS in the treatment group were lower than those in the control group, and the pain degree was less than the control group, the difference was statistically significant (P < 0.05). In the treatment group, the incidence of hyperalgesia after general anesthesia was significantly lower than that in the control group, with a significant difference (P < 0.05). Conclusion: Transcutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation can significantly reduce the incidence of hyperalgesia after remifentanil general anesthesia, relieve postoperative pain and improve comfort during general anesthesia recovery.
