AB  -  INTRODUCTION: Obstetric patients who receive regional anesthesia for elective cesarean section (CS) frequently experience intraoperative nausea and vomiting. Prophylactic therapy with antiemetic agents can carry multiple adverse effects to the mother and baby. One non‐pharmacological method, transcutaneous P6 acupoint stimulation, has been found to be effective in reduction of intra‐operative nausea. However, to our knowledge, no published clinical trials have found this method to be effective in reducing intraoperative vomiting during CS under combined spinal epidural (CSE). We conducted a prospective non‐blinded randomized control clinical trial to compare no prophylactic antiemetic treatment and no transcutaneous P6 acupoint stimulation versus intravenous metoclopramide and ondansetron versus transcutaneous P6 acupoint stimulation for prophylactic antiemetic treatment for CS under CSE. METHODS: After obtaining IRB approval and patient consent, 180 patients were enrolled into this prospective randomized control clinical trial, with 60 patients in each group. The control group received no antiemetic therapy and no acupoint P6 stimulation. The pharmacological group received intravenous metoclopramide and ondansetron. The P6 stimulation group received transcutaneous P6 acupoint electrical stimulation on the right wrist via an FDA approved SunMed Peripheral Nerve Stimulator (Figure 1). Evidence of nausea and vomiting was collected intraoperatively. Satisfaction scales for nausea and vomiting treatment and overall satisfaction were recorded (0 = no satisfaction; 10 = best satisfaction). IBM SPSS Statistics V22.0 was used for Chi‐squared test, Fisher's exact test, and ANOVA analysis of our data. Data was presented as mean ± standard deviation or percentages, and a P value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. RESULTS: All 180 parturients completed the study and were comparable with respect to parturients' age, gestational age, height, weight, and intraoperative experience of hypertension, hypotension, hypoxia, blood loss and surgical duration time (Table 1). Parturients experienced statistically significantly more nausea intraoperatively in the control group (73.3%) than in the pharmacologic group (23.3%) and the P6 stimulation group (36.7%, P < 0.001). There was no statistically significant difference in nausea experienced between the pharmacological and P6 stimulation groups (Figure 2). Parturients also vomited markedly more intraoperatively in the control group (45%) than in the pharmacological group (16.7%) and the P6 stimulation group (13.3%, P < 0.001). There was no statistically significant difference in the vomiting experienced between the pharmacological and P6 stimulation groups. (Figure 3). Parturients in the control group were less satisfied than parturients in pharmacological group with their anti‐emetic treatment (80% vs 95%, P = 0.03). No statistical significant difference was found between pharmacological and P6 stimulation groups in terms of anti‐emetic treatment satisfaction and overall anesthetic management satisfaction (Table 3). CONCLUSIONS: Transcutaneous P6 acupoint electrical stimulation was found to be a simple, non‐invasive, effective alternative antiemetic treatment that could be of great interest to patients and obstetric anesthesiologists who prefer less invasive care with fewer side effects, especially life‐threatening side effects like ventricular tachycardia, for cesarean section performed under regional anesthesia. (Figure Presented) .
