IS  -  12
CC  -  Fertility Regulation
SP  -  1673‐1674
M3  -  Journal article
AB  -  Objective: To evaluate the effect of percutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation on perioperative pain, anxiety and intraoperative abortion syndrome in induced abortion patients. Methods: 240 induced abortion patients were randomly divided into two groups. The observation group received percutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation with lidocaine plus paracervical nerve block before operation. The control group received only 2% lidocaine paracervical nerve block anesthesia. The incidence of intraoperative abortion syndrome was compared between the two groups, and pain and anxiety scale scores were used to compare preoperative and postoperative pain and anxiety scores. Results: Postoperative pain, anxiety scores and the incidence of abortion syndrome in the control group were significantly higher than those in the observation group (P<0.05). The postoperative anxiety score of the observation group was significantly lower than that before operation (P<0.05). Conclusion: Preoperative percutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation can reduce the pain and anxiety of induced abortion patients, and reduce the incidence of abortion syndrome.
