AU  -  ChiCTR2500110428,
TI  -  A Double-Blind Randomized Controlled Trial Protocol of Transcutaneous Electrical Acupoint Stimulation for Preventing Post-TURP Complications: the TEAS-PRO Trial
JA  -  https://trialsearch.who.int/Trial2.aspx?TrialID=ChiCTR2500110428
PY  -  2025
C3  -  ICTRP ChiCTR2500110428
M3  -  Trial registry record
AB  -  INTERVENTION: Blank control group:Following post‐TURP nursing guidelines, routine postoperative care includes the following: 1. Monitoring of Vital Signs and Condition: In the early postoperative period, closely monitor the patient’s level of consciousness, temperature, pulse, respiration, blood pressure, and oxygen saturation. Regularly assess the patient’s subjective complaints, such as the nature, location, and intensity of pain. Observe abdominal signs and the recovery of bowel motility. Record 24‐hour fluid intake and output, with particular attention to changes in urine output. 2. Management of Continuous Bladder Irrigation (CBI) System: Use sterile normal saline or a prescribed irrigation solution. Maintain the irrigation fluid temperature close to body temperature (approximately 34–38°C) to minimize the risk of bladder spasms caused by excessively cold fluids. Adjust the irrigation rate dynamically based on the color of the drainage fluid. In the early postoperative phase or when the drainage is dark red, maintain a higher irrigation rate to ensure unobstructed flow and prevent clot formation. As the drainage color gradually lightens to pale red or pink, reduce the irrigation rate according to physician orders (e.g., 60–90 drops per minute) to maintain a pale red or clear drainage, while avoiding excessive irrigation that may lead to bladder overdistension and spasms. 3. Maintenance of Catheter Patency: Regularly squeeze the drainage tubing to ensure unobstructed flow and prevent blockage by blood clots. Ensure the irrigation circuit remains sealed, free of kinks, compression, or dislodgement. 4. Observation of Drainage Fluid: Closely observe and document the color, consistency, and volume of drainage fluid. Indications for discontinuing continuous irrigation: When th CONDITION: Benign prostatic hyperplasia PRIMARY OUTCOME: Incidence and Severity of Bladder Spasms;Infection Status;Bleeding condition; SECONDARY OUTCOME: Serum/urine inflammatory cytokines, including IL‐6 and TNF‐a;Neurotransmitters, including substance P and ß‐endorphin;Pathological biopsy of the bladder mucosa; INCLUSION CRITERIA: 1. Patients aged 50–80 years undergoing transurethral resection of the prostate; 2. Requiring continuous bladder irrigation for >=24 hours postoperatively; 3. Development of at least one bladder irrigation‐related complication within 48 hours postoperatively; 4. Voluntary participation in the study with signed written informed consent; 5. Preoperative International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS) >=8 points without concomitant severe bladder dysfunction.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02925750/full
ER  -  


