AB  -  Purpose: Colonoscopy is often regarded as a painful procedure. Acu‐TENS is a new and non‐invasive treatment modality for musculoskeletal pain, but few studies have evaluated its role in treating pain during colonoscopy. Using an electronic barostat as an experimental pain model to standardize and mimic visceral pain caused by gaseous distension during colonoscopy, we sought to evaluate the efficacy and mechanism of Acu‐ TENS in visceral pain reduction. Methods: Forty consecutive asymptomatic participants were randomized to receive either 45 minutes of Acu‐ TENS or placebo‐TENS to acupoints Zusanli, Hegu, and Neiguan before the barostat procedure. Phasic isobaric rectal distension ranged from 0 to 48 mmHg was used to induce discomfort. Discomfort/pain scores on a visual analogue scale (VAS) at different inflation pressures, as well as perception/pain thresholds reported by the participants were recorded and compared between two groups. Peripheral blood samples were taken before, during, and after the Acu‐TENS/barostat procedure to determine β‐ endorphin levels. Results: The demographic data and the baseline plasma β‐endorphin levels of the two groups were comparable. More participants in the Acu‐TENS group could tolerate higher distension pressures (>40 mmHg) than the placebo group (65% vs. 15%, P=0.003). The mean VAS pain scores of the Acu‐TENS group were lower than that of the placebo group at all inflation pressures, but the difference was not statistically significant. The plasma β‐endorphin levels of the Acu‐TENS group were significantly higher than that of the placebo group during the barostat procedure at an inflation pressure of 24 mmHg (1.31+/‐0.40 ng/ml vs. 1.04+/‐0.43 ng/ml, P=0.044) and after the barostat procedure (1.46+/‐0.53 ng/ml vs. 0.95+/‐0.38 ng/ml, P=0.003). Conclusions: Participants receiving Acu‐TENS have better tolerance to the distension discomfort/pain induced by the rectal barostat. The analgesic effect of Acu‐TENS is closely related to the release of β‐endorphin in blood. The role of Acu‐TENS in treating pain during real colonoscopy warrants further investigation.
