AB  -  Objectives: to observe the therapeutic effect of external application and enema of herbal medicine combined with acupuncture for adhesive obstruction. Methods: 64 AIO patients were randomized into three groups by random number. Group A received routine western medical treatment including gastrointestinal decompression anti‐inflammation and supporting therapy. Group B waas treated with electro‐acupuncture(EA) on bilateral Zusanli acupoints, and group C with EA, retention enema with Tongfu Xiere mixture and external application of Shuangbai Powder on abdominal painful sites. Pain‐disappearing time, anus‐bubbling time, obstruction relieving time and drainage volume from nutrient canal during gastrointestinal decompression were investigated in the three groups. Results: there were 3 patients inverting to open surgery in group A, 3 in group B and 2 in group C. The other 56 patients did not receive surgery but were cured. Obstruction‐relieving rate was 85.0% in group A, 85.7% in group B and 91.3% in group C (p>0.05), pain‐disappreaing time, anus‐bubbling time, and the time for gas‐fluid plane disappearing were shorted in group C, and drainage volume from nutrient canal during gastrointestinal decompression obviously reduced on the second day as compared with those in groups A and B (P<0.05 or P<0.01). Conclusion: comprehensive therapy of external application and enema of herbal medicine combined with acupuncture shortens obstruction‐relieving time and promotes recovery, indicating that it is effective for AIO.
