AB  -  Background and Objective(s): To reduce the spasticity, Current treatments of gastrocnemius muscle tone comprise physical therapy, pharmacological interventions (such as botulinum toxin injections) and surgeries for resolving the problem of spasms. There was no study distinguishing the different between the effects of electro‐acupuncture (EA) and acupuncture on children with spastic cerebral palsy (CP). To further verify this construction, we designed an experimental study of EA to relieve gastrocnemius muscle tone for children with spastic CP. Study Design: Randomized controlled. Study Participants and Settings: A total of thirty‐six children with spastic CP, aged 24‐60 months (23 boys, 13 girls), were receiving rehabilitation treatment (the developmental behavioral pediatrics, the First Hospital of Jilin University) from December 2016 to January 2018. Materials and Methods: through a table of random numbers for random grouping to EA group and acupuncture group. The EA group received 30min/d of the EA intervention; The acupuncture group received 30min/d of the acupuncture intervention. Acupoints include Zusanli (ST36), Shangjuxu (ST37), Sanyinjiao (SP6), Xuanzhong (GB39). The root mean square (RMS) and integreted electromyogram (iEMG) of the gastrocnemius of surface electromyography (sEMG), Modified Tardieu Scale (MTS) were used to evaluate the immediate effects of EA and acupuncture in gastrocnemius muscle tone of children with spastic CP. Results: The proportion of boys was 67% (n=12) in the EA group and 61% (n=11) in the acupuncture group, showing a higher proportion of boys than girls in each group, with no significant differences across the groups (Table 1). There was also no significant difference in age, with a mean age of 40.2±7.5 months in EA group and 40.2±10.4 months in acupuncture group. After the treatment, the passive drafting under RMS, iEMG and MTS value in both of the two groups were significantly reduced (p<0.05, p<0.05, p<0.05). IEMG no significant difference between the two groups after treatment (p>0.05). Compared with the acupuncture group, EA group showed significant improvements in RMS and MTS (p<0.05, p<0.05). Conclusions or Significance: It's the first time to demonstrate the immediate effects of EA in gastrocnemius muscle tone of children with spastic CP. The results of this study confirm that acupuncture and EA both can relieve the gastrocnemius muscle tone in spastic CP, and EA may be better.
