AB  -  Purpose: The primary purpose of this study is to explore whether electroacupuncture (EA) in combination with usual‐care (UC) can provide benefits to patients with non‐acute pain and dysfunction after back surgery. It is also a pilot feasibility study that is designed to estimate the appropriate sample size for a future confirmative, pragmatic, comparative randomized controlled trial (RCT) that would verify the effectiveness of EA in combination with UC compared with UC alone in relieving non‐acute pain and dysfunction after back surgery. Methods: This study is a pilot randomized, active‐controlled, assessor‐ blinded trial. Patients with recurring or persistent low back pain after back surgery, defined as a visual analogue scale (VAS) value of ≥50 mm, with or without leg pain, are randomly assigned to an EA plus UC group or to a UC only group. Patients assigned to both groups have UC management, including physical therapy and patient education, twice a week during a 4‐week treatment period that would begin at randomization. Patients assigned to the EA plus UC group also have EA twice a week during the 4‐week treatment period. The primary outcome is measured with the 100mm pain VAS of low back pain by a blinded evaluator. The outcomes are measured before the start of treatment at each visit, and follow‐up assessment is performed at 4 and 8 weeks after 4‐week treatment period. Results: Thirty patients (EA plus UC; 12, UC; 18) were included in the per‐protocol analysis. Over the 4 weeks treatment period, there were significant differences in VAS scores (P = 0.01) between EA plus UC group (‐33.7 ± 20.6) and UC alone group (‐9.3 ± 14.7). Conclusion: The primary outcome measurement allowed the sample size to be calculated for a future randomized controlled trial. Considering about 20% of dropout rate, a sample size of 26 patients (13 in each arms) is required.
