AB  -  Fourteen patients having nodular goiter with normal thyroid functions were studied to evaluate the effect of electrical acupuncture on thyroid function as judged by plasma thyroid hormone levels during extirpation of the nodular goiters under neuroleptic anesthesia. They were devided at random into two groups. Eight patients served as the control group who received neuroleptic anesthesia composed of droperidol, fentanyl and nitrous oxide (70%) supplemented by a muscle relaxant. Stainless needles were put into bilateral Futotsu or Tentei and electrical stimulation of 1 Hz was given with Tokki pI 200 throughout the operative procedure. Plasma levels of thyroxine (T4), triidothyronine (T3) and reverse triidothyronine (rT3) were estimated ten times by means of radioimmunoassay before, during and after the operative procedure. Plasma T4 levels were unchanged throughout the procedure, but T3 levels decreased significantly postoperatively which was followed by a marked increase in plasma rT3 levels. There was no statistical significant difference in plasma T4, T3 and rT3 levels between both groups at any time. The results imply that electrical acupuncture in combination with neuroleptic anesthesia has no significant effect on thyroid function during extirpation of the nodular goiter.
