AB  -  INTERVENTION: Electrical acupuncture will be applied to the arms and legs. During the treatment, electrical current delivered with a battery‐operated eletrical stimulator is combined with acupuncture needle techniques to enhance stimulation of the acupuncture points. Each treatment session takes 25 to 30 minutes. The treatment will be given twice a week for four weeks followed by once a week for two weeks then once every two weeks for four weeks. In total, 12 sessions are delivered within 10 weeks. Participants in this group will also receive pain and medication management (i.e., standard care). CONDITION: Alternative and Complementary Medicine ‐ Other alternative and complementary medicine chronic musculoskeletal pain;use of opioid medications; ; chronic musculoskeletal pain ; use of opioid medications Musculoskeletal ‐ Other muscular and skeletal disorders Other ‐ Conditions of unknown or disputed aetiology (such as chronic fatigue syndrome/myalgic encephalomyelitis) PRIMARY OUTCOME: The weekly dosage of opioid medicaitons (OM). This will be calculated based on the data recorded by the pariticipants in their Pain and Medication Diary.[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation.] INCLUSION CRITERIA: 1) age between 18 and 85 years at entry; 2) confident in conversational and reading English; 3) suffering from chronic musculoskeletal pain (CMP), regardless of the locations of pain; and 4) have taken opioid medication (OM) regularly for more than two months without dose limitation. SECONDARY OUTCOME: 1) Number of participants who achieve 50% OM reduction[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation; and ; at the end of the follow‐up period, i.e. three months after the end of the treatment.] 10) The proportion of neuropathic versus nociceptive pain assessed with the Self‐administered Leeds Assessment of Neuropathic Symptoms and Signs Pain Scale (S‐LANSS)[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation] 11) The Perception of EA Treatment Questionnaire will be given to the two electroacupuncture groups to assess the success of blinding[In the 6th and 14th weeks, i.e. two and 10 weeks following randomisation.] 2) Type, severity and incidence of OM related common adverse events (AEs). Common AEs are somnolence, mental clouding, nausea and constipation. They are assessed with six‐point scales (0 = none, 1 = minimal, 2 = mild, 3 = moderate, 4 = severe, and 5 = extremely severe) to indicate the severity of these events. The scales are included at the end of Pain and Medication Diary that is completed by the participants.[at the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation] 3) Pain intensity measured with Visual Analogue Scales (VASs; 0 = no pain; 10 = worst pain possible) and with the numerical scales included in the Brief Pain Inventory[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation; and ; at the end of the follow‐up period, i.e. three months after the end of the treatment.] 4) Consumption of non‐opioid medications that are recorded by the participants in their Pain and Medication Diary. To make meaningful comparisons of non‐opioid medications use, the dosage of such medications will be calculated according to Medication Quantification Scale Version III (MQS).[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation; and ; at the end of the follow‐up period, i.e. three months after the end of the treatment.] 5) Quality of life assessed with SF‐36v2 Health Survey[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation; and ; at the end of the follow‐up period, i.e. three months after the end of the treatment.] 6) participants' attitude of paina as assessed with Survey of Patients Attitude.[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation; and ; at the end of the follow‐up period, i.e. three months after the end of the treatment.] 7) The level of function assessed with the Roland‐Morris Disability Questionnaire (RMDQ) and the Brief Pain Inventory;[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation; and ; at the end of the follow‐up period, i.e. three months after the end of the treatment.] 8) The presentation and severity of withdrawal symptoms assessed with Short Opiate Withdrawal Scale.[At the end of the treatment period, i.e. 10 weeks following randomisation]
