TI  -  The Effect of Preoperative Electroacupuncture and Acupuncture on Post-Hysterectomy Nausea and Vomiting
JA  -  https://trialsearch.who.int/Trial2.aspx?TrialID=IRCT20200206046395N7
PY  -  2025
C3  -  ICTRP IRCT20200206046395N7
M3  -  Trial registry record
AB  -  INTERVENTION: Intervention 1: Intervention Group 1 (Manual Acupuncture) Participants in this group will receive manual acupuncture at the standard PC6 (Neiguan) and ST36 (Zusanli) points. The PC6 point is located approximately 2 cm (about 3 fingerbreadths) proximal to the wrist crease on the inner forearm. The ST36 point is located 3 cm (about 4 fingerbreadths) below the lower border of the patella and one fingerbreadth lateral to the anterior crest of the tibia. After skin disinfection, stainless steel needles (0.30 × 40 mm) will be inserted into these points until the patient reports a De Qi sensation (heaviness, pressure, or mild tingling). The needles will be retained for 30 minutes prior to surgery without any electrical stimulation. Intervention 2: Intervention Group 2 (Electroacupuncture) Participants in this group will receive electroacupuncture. Needle insertion at the standard PC6 and ST36 points will be performed identically to the Manual Acupuncture group. Following needle insertion and the elicitation of De Qi, electrodes from an XS‐998B electroacupuncture device will be connected between the PC6 and ST36 points. Variable frequency electrical stimulation (20‐100 Hz) will be applied for 30 minutes prior to surgery. The current intensity will be adjusted to a level perceived as strong but not painful by the patient. Intervention 3: Control Group (Sham Acupuncture) Participants in the control group will receive sham acupuncture. Non‐penetrating, placebo needles will be placed superficially at non‐specific points located approximately 2 cm away from the standard PC6 (Neiguan) and ST36 (Zusanli) acupuncture points. The needles will be inserted superficially without deep insertion or any form of stimulation (manual or electrical) and will remain in place for 30 minutes prior t CONDITION: K91.0 Postoperative nausea and vomiting. ; Vomiting following gastrointestinal surgery PRIMARY OUTCOME: Nausea Severity. Timepoint: It will be evaluated and documented at 4, 8, 12, and 24 hours after surgery. Method of measurement: Assessment using a 100‐millimeter Visual Analogue Scale where patients mark their symptom intensity on a straight line. Need for Postoperative Antiemetic Drugs. Timepoint: 0–4 hours, 4–8 hours, 8–12 hours, and 12–24 hours after surgery. Method of measurement: Counting the number of patients who received antiemetic drugs and their dosage. The Prevalence of Postoperative Nausea and Vomiting (PONV). Timepoint: At 4, 8, 12, and 24 hours after surgery. Method of measurement: Direct questioning of patients using the following questions:'Did you feel nauseous at any time since your last visit?''Did you vomit during this period?. INCLUSION CRITERIA: Women aged 18 to 60 years American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) physical status class I or II Body mass inde X(BMI) between 20 and 30 Non‐smokers Candidates for hysterectomy under general anesthesia No history of nausea or vomiting within 24 hours prior to surgery
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02943513/full
ER  -  


