AB  -  Objective : To observe the influence of electroacupuncture‐associated general anesthesia on use of venous analgetic pump in abdominal postoperative patients .Method : Forty cases using analgetic pump after date‐chosen biliary passage operation were randomly divided into a control group(simple general anesthesia)and an electroacupuncture group (electroacupuncture‐associated general anesthesia).Recorded were intraoperative anesthetic dosage , analgetic dosage during postoperative 6 h , 24 h and 48 h and the times of postoperative anal flatus and defecation.Results: Fentanyl and propofol dosages during operation and analgetic dosage during postoperative 6 h both decreased in the electroacupuncture group as compared to the control group and there was a significant difference(P <0 .05).But there were no significant differences in intraoperative atracurium dosage , analgetic dosage during postoperative 24 h and 48 h and the time of postoperative recovery of intestinal peristalsis.Conclusion: Electroacupuncture‐associated general anesthesia can improve an analgetic effect but does not significantly influence postoperative recovery of intestinal peristalsis.
