AB  -  INTERVENTION: Electroacupuncture analgesia group,Control group Patients underwent total thyroidectomy with electroacupuncture analgesia. An experienced acupuncturist administered acupuncture at five acupuncture points: Hegu, Neiguan, Shuitu, Quepen, and Yifeng. Needles were conntected to an electroacupuncture stimulator. Stimulation began 25 minutes before the surgery and stopped when the surgery finished.,A three‐point injection technique was used for bilateral superficial cervical nerve block with an injection of lignocaine per side, preoperatively. The volume of lignocaine was divided equally among three points on one side. The injections targeted the sternocleidomastoid muscle at its posterior border midpoint, distributing the lignocaine in cephalic, medial, and caudal directions. Treatment,Treatment CONDITION: Electroacupuncture analgesia in thyroidectomy for patients with Graves disease ; electroacupuncture; acupuncture analgesia; Graves disease; thryoidectomy; beta‐endorphin electroacupuncture; acupuncture analgesia; Graves disease; thryoidectomy; beta‐endorphin PRIMARY OUTCOME: Level of analgesia During the surgery On a pain scale,Perioperative blood pressure During the surgery mmHg,Intraoperative heart rate During the surgery beats per minute,Intraoperative respiratory rate During the surgery Breaths per minute,Intraoperative oxygen saturation During the surgery % SECONDARY OUTCOME: Pain threshold Before after electroacupuncture starts for 25 minutes grams per second,Beta‐endorphin Before and after electroacupuncture starts for 25 minutes picograms per millilitre INCLUSION CRITERIA: Age 16 years or more Graves disease requiring thyroidectomy
