AB  -  INTERVENTION: Treat group:The operation and treatment were performed using Hua Tuo disposable needle (0.30×40mm) and SDZ‐V electroacupuncture stimulator (Suzhou Medical Instrument Co., LTD., Suzhou, China). Thirty minutes before the operation, the main acupoints for the patients upper limb on the surgical side were selected as Neiguan (PC6) + Hegu (LI4), and the complementary acupoints were Quchi (LI11) + Shize (LU5). The location of the acupoints was strictly carried out in accordance with the National standard of the People's Republic of China "Acupoint Locations GB12346‐90". After routine disinfection by the acupunccian, A single‐handed needle insertion method is used for direct needling. After performing the technique of tonifying and purging to obtain qi, the A channel of the electrical stimulator is connected to Neiguan (+) and Hegu (‐), and the B channel is connected to Quchi (+) and Chize (‐). The frequency of the waves is selected (stimulation parameters:) The frequency of electrical stimulation is 2 Hz/100 Hz, with an intensity of 0.3‐0.5mA, which should be tolerated by the patient. The duration of electrical stimulation should refer to the first part of the experiment (Table 1). After the electrical stimulation, ultrasound was used to measure the inner diameter of the patients blood vessels and Doppler blood flow parameters (PSV, EDV, Vmean, VFR, PI, RI).;Control group:On the basis of conventional treatment, blank control therapy was performed using the simulated electroacupuncture method. The selection of acupuncture needles and acupoints was the same as that of the treatment group. First, cover the patients acupoints with micro‐porous medical tape. Place the acupuncture needles in the holes of the medical tape (without penetrating the skin). After 1 minute, connect the CONDITION: Radial artery spasm after interventional treatment of intracranial aneurysm PRIMARY OUTCOME: Long‐term reuse rate of the radial artery; SECONDARY OUTCOME: The severity of radial artery spasm;Arterial Doppler blood flow parameters;The duration and frequency of radial artery puncture after acupuncture;The utilization rate of nitroglycerin;Incidence of intraoperative hypotension;Delium;The incidence of acute embolic events;Changes in the five coagulation items and inflammatory factors;Patient and surgeon satisfaction scores; INCLUSION CRITERIA: (1) Aged 18 to 75, BMI<28kg/m ²; (2) ASA classification grades I ‐ III; (3) Elective interventional surgery for intracranial aneurysms via the radial artery approach is performed; (4) Preoperative upper limb ultrasound shows that at least one of the bilateral radial arteries can be used to establish a surgical access. (5) Radial artery patency assessment: Barbeau test type A‐C; (6) Surgeries with a duration of less than 2 to 4 hours; (7) Be willing to sign the informed consent form.
