AB  -  ACKGROUND&amp;AIMS: Electroacupuncture (EA) may reduce the duration of postoperative ileus (POI) after laparoscopic gastrectomy for gastric cancer, though evidence is limited. We investigated the efficacy of EA in reducing POI duration and enhancing gastrointestinal (GI) recovery in patients undergoing laparoscopic surgery for gastric cancer. METHODS: This multicenter randomized trial was conducted at seven tertiary hospitals in China, enrolling 585 participants who underwent laparoscopic resection for gastric cancer from October 27, 2021, to December 21, 2023. Participants received perioperative standard care and were randomized to EA, sham EA (SA), or standard care only. EA and SA protocols consisted of 4 acupuncture sessions within 72 hours postoperatively, followed by a 4‐week post‐treatment follow‐up. The primary outcome was the time to first flatus. Secondary outcomes included time to first defecation, dietary recovery, quality of life, motor function, and duration of hospital stay. RESULTS: This trial included 578 participants in the full analysis set. The EA group exhibited a shorter time to first flatus compared to both SA and standard care groups, with differences of ‐12.96 (95% CI, ‐19.17 to ‐6.75; P < 0.001) and ‐24.46 (95% CI, ‐30.61 to ‐18.30; P < 0.001), respectively. For the time to first defecation, EA also demonstrated a significant reduction: ‐15.41 (95% CI, 27.73 to ‐3.09; P = 0.007) versus SA; ‐24.66 (95% CI, ‐36.76 to ‐12.55; P < 0.001) versus standard care only. Furthermore, the incidence of prolonged postoperative ileus was significantly lower in the electroacupuncture group, with differences of ‐0.41 (95% CI, ‐0.66 to ‐0.16; P < 0.001) and ‐0.56 (95% CI, ‐0.82 to ‐0.31; P < 0.001), respectively. No severe adverse events were reported. CONCLUSIONS: EA was superior to SA and standard care only in enhancing early GI recovery, decreasing the risk of prolonged POI, and improving the quality of life for patients undergoing laparoscopic gastrectomy for gastric cancer (Trial number: ChiCTR2100050660).
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02926252/full
