AB  -  Purpose: This cost‐effectiveness analysis aimed to evaluate the difference in cost and health outcome of adding or not adding acupuncture treatment to usual care in controlling postlaparotomy pain of gynecological patients in Hong Kong. Methods: Data collected in a randomized controlled trial of 72 gynecological laparotomy patients from 2016‐2018 was used. The health effects were measured using the Short Form ‐ 6 ‐ dimension and verified by the EuroQol ‐ 5 Dimension ‐ 5 Response from after surgery to postoperative day 5. Direct costs from health care providers' perspective were estimated. A costreduction analysis and sensitivity analyses were performed. Results: The improvement in the mean number of quality adjusted life years in acupuncture group over the control group was insignificant. The mean (standard deviation) total cost for the acupuncture group (HKD 26,014.6 (8695.0)) (HKD 1 = USD 0.13) was significantly lower than that of the control group (HKD 26,491.7 (9,270.2)), saving HKD 477.1 per patient (P = 0.042). Conclusion: Adding the acupuncture treatment may save HKD 477.1 per patient over providing only usual care, with an insignificant improvement in quality adjusted life years.
