AB  -  Objective: To observe the analgesic effects of different electroacupuncture methods in artificial abortion. Methods: 120 patients who voluntarily requested artificial abortion were randomly divided into groups A, B, C and D, with 30 cases in each group. Group A was treated with electroacupuncture during induced abortion, group B was treated with percutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation, group C was treated with non‐meridian and non‐acupoint electroacupuncture, and group D did not take any analgesic measures. Observe the changes of various hemodynamic indicators [mean arterial pressure (MAP), heart rate (HR), blood oxygen saturation (SPO2)] in the four groups of patients during the operation; evaluate the visual simulation of pain during operation and 30 minutes after operation Score (VAS); Record the cervical laxity and the occurrence of induced abortion syndrome during the operation. Results: There was no statistically significant difference in the hemodynamic indicators (MAP, HR and SPO2) between the four groups at different time points (P>0.05). There were statistically significant differences in the VAS scores of group A and group B during operation and 30 minutes after operation compared with group C and group D (P<0.05). The postoperative cervical laxity grades of group A and group B were compared with group C and group D, and the difference was statistically significant (P<0.05). The grade of postoperative abortion syndrome in group A was significantly different from that in group C (P<0.05); the difference in grade of postoperative abortion syndrome in group B was statistically significant compared with that in groups C and D (P<0.05) ). Conclusion: Both electroacupuncture and percutaneous acupoint electrical stimulation can play an analgesic effect during artificial abortion, help dilate the cervix, and reduce the incidence of postoperative abortion syndrome.
