AB  -  INTERVENTION: Test group:1. Electroacupuncture scheme 1) Acupoint selection: Select the cervical spine acupoint (0.5 inches below the spinous process of the cervical spine) based on the affected nerve root segment, supplemented by symptom related acupoints (such as brachial plexus, Quchi, etc.). 2) Operation: Insert a 25mm angled needle and connect it to an electroacupuncture device (continuous wave, 2Hz, 1‐2mA) after obtaining gas. Leave the needle on for 20 minutes, once every other day, for a course of 2 weeks. 2 rTMS scheme 1) Parameters: 5Hz frequency, 80% exercise threshold intensity, 1200 pulse sequence (stimulation 4s/interval 4s), single session for 8 minutes. 2) Target: Stimulation of the contralateral M1 area, 3 times a week, for a course of 2 weeks.;Control group:1. Electroacupuncture: Same as the experimental group. 2. Fake rTMS: coil tilt of 90 ° (ineffective stimulation), parameters/course of treatment the same as the experimental group. 3. Conventional treatment: Both groups received physical therapy (such as microwave therapy).; CONDITION: cervical spondylosis PRIMARY OUTCOME: VAS Visual Pain Analog Scale (VAS); SECONDARY OUTCOME: Neck Disability Inde X(NDI) scoring method;Tanaka Yasuhisa Cervical Disease Symptom Scale Score (20 point scale score);Zung Self Rating Anxiety Scale;Near Infrared Brain Functional Imaging (fNIRS);Surface electromyography; INCLUSION CRITERIA: 1. Meets the Western diagnostic criteria for cervical spondylosis, with neck pain as the main complaint. 2. The duration of the disease is not limited. 3. Age >= 20 and <= 75 years old, gender not limited. 4. When included, the visual analog scale score for neck pain severity was = 30 (0‐100mm). 5. Understand and agree to participate in this study and sign the informed consent form.
