AB  -  INTERVENTION: Procedure/Surgery : The intervention group (herbal medicine strategy group) is part of a pragmatic randomized controlled trial designed to evaluate the effectiveness of a herbal medicine‐based treatment strategy for chronic low back pain. The type of herbal prescription, dosing interval, and quantity are determined at the discretion of the Korean medicine doctor based on the patient’s symptoms, imaging findings, and clinical progress. For each prescription, the name, composition (in grams per ingredient), total amount (number of doses, packs, days), fixed volume per pack (per prescription), and daily dosing frequency are recorded. Participants visit the clinic every 10 days for a total of 3 visits, and are prescribed a 10‐day supply of herbal medicine at each visit, resulting in a total of 30 days of oral administration. The control group (standard Korean medicine treatment group) receives a combination of acupuncture, electroacupuncture, infrared therapy, cupping (wet/dry), moxibustion, and physical therapy (ICT or TENS), all administered based on clinical judgment. For acupuncture and electroacupuncture, treatment points and whether each modality was applied are recorded. Acupuncture uses sterile disposable needles (0.25×30mm) inserted at 10–20 acupoints selected from Shenshu, Dachangshu, Yaoyangguan, Mingmen, Ciliao, Weizhong, Kunlun, Huantiao, Yanglingquan, and Sanyinjiao, with retention for 10–15 minutes. Electroacupuncture is applied to 4 points at 3 Hz intensity up to the maximum muscle contraction level without pain. Physical therapy, moxibustion (43 ± 1 °C), and cupping are applied for 10–15 minutes each. Treatment is performed twice per week for 5 weeks, but may be adjusted to 1–3 times per week depending on the patients condition, with a maximum of 10 sessio CONDITION: Diseases of the musculoskeletal system and connective tissue PRIMARY OUTCOME: Low back pain Numeric Rating Scale, NRS Oswestry Disability Index, ODI SECONDARY OUTCOME: 5‐Level EuroQol‐5 Dimension, EQ‐5D‐5L Adverse events Drug consumption Health‐related Quality of Life Instrument with 8 Items, HINT‐8 Low back pain and radiating leg pain Visual Analogue Scale, VAS Patient Global Impression of Change, PGIC Patient Health Questionnaire‐15, PHQ‐15 Radiating leg pain Numeric Rating Scale, NRS Roland‐Morris Disability Questionnaire‐6, RMDQ‐6 the Work Productivity and Activity Impairment questionnaire, WPAI INCLUSION CRITERIA: ? Presence of low back pain persisting for more than 3 months ? Numeric Rating Scale (NRS) score for low back pain of 5 or higher ? Patients aged 19 years or older and younger than 70 years ? Individuals who agree to participate in the clinical trial and provide written informed consent
