AB  -  INTERVENTION: Experimental Procedure/Surgery,Active Comparator Procedure/Surgery Participants in this group will receive thread embedding acupuncture with absorbable polydioxanone threads at 10 specific acupoints on the affected side. The procedure will be performed once every two weeks for a total of two sessions over four weeks. All participants will also receive standard care including core‐stabilizing exercises, lifestyle modifications, and paracetamol for rescue analgesia.,Participants in this group will receive electroacupuncture at the same 10 acupoints used in the experimental group. Sterile, single‐use acupuncture needles will be used, and electrical stimulation will be applied using a continuous wave with mixed frequencies of 2‐100 Hz for 20 minutes. The sessions will be performed once daily, 5 days per week, for a total of 20 sessions over four weeks. Standard care with exercises, lifestyle modifications, and rescue paracetamol is also provided. TEA,EA CONDITION: Chronic sciatica due to lumbar disc herniation ; Sciatica; Acupuncture Sciatica; Acupuncture PRIMARY OUTCOME: Change in radiating leg pain intensity At baseline, week 2, and week 4 11‐point Numeric Rating Scale (NRS) SECONDARY OUTCOME: Change in lumbar pain intensity At baseline, week 2, and week 4 11‐point Numeric Rating Scale (NRS),Change in disability level At baseline, week 2, and week 4 Oswestry Disability Inde X(ODI) questionnaire,Number of 500 mg paracetamol tablets used weekly At week 2 and week 4 Patient self‐reported diary,Patient&amp;#039;s global assessment of treatment efficacy At week 4 Patient Global Impression of Change (PGIC) scale,Patient satisfaction with treatment At week 4 100mm Visual Analog Scale (VAS),Adverse event During four weeks Patient interviews, medical records, and clinical observations INCLUSION CRITERIA: 1. Aged between 18 and 65 years. 2. Diagnosed with unilateral sciatica lasting more than 3 months due to lumbar disc herniation compressing the S1 nerve root, confirmed by Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI). 3. Moderate to severe radiating leg pain with an NRS score of 4 or higher. 4. Willing to participate and having provided written informed consent.
